The list must be cut at its first zero into the part before that zero and the part from the zero onward, returned as a tuple; with no zero, the whole list is the first part and the second is empty.

The implementation:

        for idx in range(len(lst)):
            if lst[idx] == 0:
                return (lst[:idx], lst[idx:])
        return (list(lst), [])

The loop visits positions in increasing order and stops at the first one holding zero, so later zeros cannot affect where the cut is made. lst[:idx] is everything strictly before that position and lst[idx:] starts at the zero itself, which gives the inclusive second half the specification asks for. When no position holds zero, the loop completes and the function returns a copy of the list together with an empty list. Slicing always builds new lists, and list(lst) does the same in the no-zero case, so the caller's list is never aliased or modified.

An input not among the visible asserts: [7, -3, 0, 2, 0]. Positions 0 and 1 hold 7 and -3, neither equal to zero, and position 2 does, so the function returns ([7, -3], [0, 2, 0]). The trailing zero stays in the second part rather than triggering another split, and the negative number is treated like any other non-zero value. An empty list gives ([], []) because the loop never runs, which fits the no-zero rule. Floating-point zero, 0.0, also compares equal to 0 and would be found. None of the three example lists are referenced in the code, so the same slicing applies to every input.

